      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The white, dusty bloom you are observing is a classic manifestation of cocoa butter migration, a phenomenon that can be likened to a goblin's mischievous attempt to hide its treasure in plain sight. In the context of your dark chocolate batches, this \"dust\" is not mold or contamination, but rather a crystallization issue where the cocoa butter has separated from the cocoa solids and migrated to the surface.\n\nTo resolve this, you must rigorously control your tempering temperatures to ensure the cocoa butter forms stable, small crystals (specifically Form V, beta-V) rather than unstable ones. If your tempering is off, the chocolate will behave like a goblin in a storm: it will melt, flow, and then re-solidify incorrectly, leading to that unsightly bloom.\n\nHere is the technical breakdown and your action plan:\n\n**1. The Science of the Bloom**\nThere are two primary types of bloom, and your description suggests **fat bloom** (cocoa butter migration). This occurs when the chocolate is not properly tempered, allowing larger, unstable crystals to form. These crystals melt at room temperature and migrate to the surface, where they recrystallize into a white, powdery layer. Alternatively",
